#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
gen_audio.py -- procedural NES (Ricoh 2A03 style) audio for the SMB-style remake.

Everything here is synthesised from scratch: oscillators, a tiny tracker /
sequencer and ORIGINAL compositions (no Nintendo melodies are transcribed or
paraphrased).  Running this script regenerates every audio asset:

    assets/audio/<name>.ogg|.mp3    sfx, jingles and music loops (one flat folder;
                                    the game tries .ogg first, then .mp3)
    assets/audio.json               documentation manifest (events, durations, bpm)
    tools/audio/check/*.png         waveform/spectrogram contact sheets (QA)

Music files are exactly one loop body (no lead-in, no tail) so they loop
seamlessly with a plain <audio loop>; the decoded .ogg length is checked to be
sample-exact.

Usage:  python3 tools/audio/gen_audio.py          (needs numpy, scipy, matplotlib, ffmpeg)

Sound model (per 2A03 channel, all values are 4-bit "DAC levels" 0..15):
  p1, p2  pulse waves, duty 12.5/25/50/75 %, band-limited with polyBLEP,
          frequencies quantised to real NES 11-bit timer periods
  tri     32-step 4-bit triangle (no volume control, only gate), used for bass
  noise   15-bit LFSR noise (long mode / short "metallic" mode), NES period table
The four channels are combined with the NES non-linear mixer formula and then
run through a gentle 40 Hz high-pass (DC block) and 14 kHz low-pass, like the
analogue output stage of the console.  No reverb.
"""

